<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matilda of Ringelheim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 892–968</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Saint Matilda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matilda of Ringelheim was the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>, and mother of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a>. Educated at the abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herford">Herford</a>, she became known for her piety and charity, founded convents including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a> and Nordhausen, and was venerated as a saint after her death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matilda-of-ringelheim.jpg" alt="Matilda of Ringelheim" width="313" height="287" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ptacnik_Mechtilda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
